Create child processes with clone() instead of fork().

wasm32-unknown-linux-musl has no fork(): the WebAssembly call stack lives
outside linear memory, so a call cannot return twice.  The platform's
process-creation primitive is clone() with an entry function -- the child gets
a private copy of the address space, so it sees every object the parent had,
including the caller's stack frame, but starts on a fresh stack of its own.

xio_fork()'s callers split cleanly in two along that line:

  * The EXEC, SYSTEM and SHELL addresses fork a sub child that never returns to
    its caller: it finishes _xioopen_foxec()'s descriptor setup and execs.  That
    is expressible as an entry function, so this patch turns the child branch
    into foxec_child() plus a per-address continuation and creates it with the
    new xio_clone().  Option "nofork" runs the same branch inline, as before.

  * The "fork" address option's child resumes the parent's call chain: it breaks
    out of the accept loop and returns up through xioopen() into socat's
    transfer loop.  Only a returning-twice fork() can do that, so xio_fork() now
    refuses instead of serving one connection and silently ceasing to listen.

sycls.c's Fork() wrapper goes away with its only caller; keeping it would leave
an undefined reference to fork().

